      "name": "grounding",
      "notes": [
        "Design fixed before running: {\"change_step_range\": [20, 30], \"changed_factor_cycle\": [\"camera\", \"camera\", \"partner\", \"mechanism\"], \"changed_lifetimes\": \"every odd-numbered lifetime (half)\", \"confidence\": 0.95, \"events_per_step\": \"camera + partner word every step; one reference reading every 4th step\", \"reading_noise\": 0.1, \"reference_readings_per_root\": 2, \"reference_root_flip_probability\": 0.15, \"steps_per_lifetime\": 40}.",
        "Each lifetime draws mechanism, camera alignment and partner convention uniformly. Camera and word readings carry 10% reading noise; each reference root is secretly inverted with probability 0.15 (the instance's prior), and each root gives 2 readings.",
        "Changed lifetimes flip one factor silently at a step drawn uniformly from 20..29 of 40; the changed factor cycles camera, camera, partner, mechanism. Unchanged lifetimes never change.",
        "Pooled arm: one epoch for the whole lifetime (the prototype behaviour). Detecting arm (the product flow, judged by the checks): after every event, beliefs.detect_change; on a non-ambiguous suspicion the executive's decision is recorded and its question answered by a truthful simulated owner with executive.resolve(confirm=True) when the asked scope really changed (at or before that event), else confirm=False; the lifetime stays in one epoch, so a confirmed camera change relearns only the camera. Manual new epoch arm (the earlier policy, kept for comparison): the question is closed and a new epoch started, so every factor is relearned. On an ambiguous suspicion both detecting arms log the executive's read-only decision (test_reference when references exist) and keep pooling.",
        "The executive check: on every non-ambiguous suspicion the executive asks about that scope or defers on it (ask budget of 3 per epoch exhausted, or the owner already answered about it with fewer than 3 new events of that scope since).",
        "Final answers are read from beliefs.compute of each arm's last epoch. Confidently wrong means P>0.95 on the wrong value. Coverage is the fraction of answers with P>0.95. Ties count 0.5 toward accuracy. Brier is (P(bit=1) - truth)^2 averaged over answers. Partner meaning preserved: in camera-change lifetimes, the partner convention's final answer is right with P>0.95. Owner questions: executive questions the owner answered per lifetime.",
        "Thresholds come from the brief (p.19); identified_or_asked_rate_min=0.80 was declared here before running.",
        "Simulated binary readings stand in for the brief's rendered pixels and tones; this does not exercise sensory decoding."
      ],
